// ============================================================================
== 3.1 The Interior: Four Layers and Two Boundaries

The earth is layered by density, and the layers can be read from the behaviour of earthquake
waves. A wave travelling through the earth changes speed and direction at two depths, and
those changes mark the boundaries.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/geo1_ch03_crust.svg",
  "Figure 3.1 The interior and the rock cycle: four layers, two discontinuities and the three rock families.", width: 98%)

#table(
  columns: (1.0fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr, 2.15fr),
  inset: (x: 5pt, y: 4.2pt),
  align: (left + horizon, center + horizon, center + horizon, left + horizon),
  stroke: 0.5pt + jamb-border,
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { jamb-navy } else if calc.odd(y) { white } else { jamb-light },
  table.header(
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Layer],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Depth],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[State],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[What it explains],
  ),
  [Crust], [0–70 km], [solid], [the continents, the ocean floor and every rock you can hold],
  [Mantle], [70–2 900 km], [solid, but able to flow], [convection currents that move the plates],
  [Outer core], [2 900–5 150 km], [liquid], [the magnetic field, generated by moving iron],
  [Inner core], [5 150–6 371 km], [solid], [the highest pressure and temperature in the planet],
)

#v(3pt)

The boundary between the crust and the mantle is the *Mohorovičić discontinuity*, usually
shortened to the Moho. The boundary between the mantle and the core is the *Gutenberg
discontinuity*. Both are detected, not seen: a wave that arrives earlier than expected has
passed through a faster layer, and the depth at which the speed changes can be calculated.

#rule-box("Why the inside is hot")[
  Two sources. *Primordial heat* is left over from the impact and compression that formed the
  planet. *Radioactive decay* of uranium, thorium and potassium releases heat continuously in
  the mantle and crust. The heat escapes by conduction through the crust and by convection in
  the mantle — the same convection that drags the plates across the surface at a few
  centimetres a year, about as fast as a fingernail grows.
]

== 3.2 Rocks: Three Families, One Cycle

A rock is an aggregate of minerals. There are thousands of minerals but only three ways for a
rock to form, and the UTME tests the three ways.

#table(
  columns: (0.95fr, 1.5fr, 1.35fr, 2.05fr),
  inset: (x: 5pt, y: 4.2pt),
  align: (left + horizon, left + horizon, left + horizon, left + horizon),
  stroke: 0.5pt + jamb-border,
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { jamb-navy } else if calc.odd(y) { white } else { jamb-light },
  table.header(
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Family],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[How it forms],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Examples],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[The examinable detail],
  ),
  [Igneous], [magma cools and solidifies], [granite, basalt, gabbro], [intrusive rocks cool slowly underground and are coarse-grained; extrusive rocks cool fast at the surface and are fine-grained],
  [Sedimentary], [weathered material is deposited, compacted and cemented], [sandstone, shale, limestone, coal], [the only family that can contain fossils, and the one that forms in layers called strata],
  [Metamorphic], [existing rock is changed by heat and pressure], [marble, slate, quartzite, gneiss], [marble from limestone, slate from shale, quartzite from sandstone — the parent is always named],
)

#v(3pt)

Igneous rock is classified by where it cooled as much as by what it contains. *Granite* cooled
slowly beneath the surface, so its crystals grew large; *basalt* erupted and cooled quickly, so
its crystals are too small to see. That single fact answers most questions about texture, and
it explains why granite is quarried for building while basalt forms the ocean floor.

#v(3pt)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[THE ROCK CYCLE IN FIVE MOVES]
  #v(4pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[
    *1.* Weathering breaks any rock into particles. \
    *2.* Erosion and deposition gather the particles in layers; compaction and cementation make
    *sedimentary* rock. \
    *3.* Burial and pressure bake it into *metamorphic* rock. \
    *4.* More heat melts it into magma. \
    *5.* The magma cools into *igneous* rock — and the cycle begins again.
  ]
]

#shortcut-box("Reading a rock question in three seconds")[
  Does the stem mention *layers, fossils or deposition*? Answer sedimentary. Does it mention
  *heat, pressure or a parent rock*? Answer metamorphic. Does it mention *cooling, magma, lava
  or crystals*? Answer igneous. The examples are simply the vocabulary that goes with each verb.
]

== 3.3 Continental Drift and Plate Tectonics

In 1912 Alfred Wegener argued that the continents had once been joined in a single landmass,
*Pangaea*, and had drifted apart. His evidence was the jigsaw fit of the coastlines, matching
fossils on opposite sides of the Atlantic, matching rock formations and ages, and evidence of
former glaciation in places now tropical.

#v(3pt)

#table(
  columns: (1.25fr, 1.55fr, 2.05fr),
  inset: (x: 5pt, y: 4.2pt),
  align: (left + horizon, left + horizon, left + horizon),
  stroke: 0.5pt + jamb-border,
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { jamb-navy } else if calc.odd(y) { white } else { jamb-light },
  table.header(
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Boundary],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[What happens],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Landforms],
  ),
  [Constructive (divergent)], [plates move apart and magma rises to fill the gap], [mid-ocean ridge, rift valley, new ocean floor],
  [Destructive (convergent)], [one plate sinks beneath another, or two collide], [trench, island arc, fold mountains, volcanoes],
  [Conservative (transform)], [plates slide past each other], [fault line, earthquakes without volcanoes],
)

#v(3pt)

Nigeria sits on the African plate, far from any active boundary, which is why the country has
no active volcano and very few earthquakes. But the plate has been moving for a very long time,
and its ancient collision with the South American plate left the crystalline basement that
underlies much of the west, while the separation that opened the Atlantic created the
sedimentary basins in which oil formed — the Benue Trough, the Chad Basin, the Sokoto Basin and
the Niger Delta.

#trap-box("Drift is not the same as plate tectonics")[
  *Continental drift* is the observation that the continents have moved. *Plate tectonics* is
  the mechanism that explains it: the lithosphere is broken into plates that move on the
  asthenosphere, driven by convection. A question that asks *what causes* the movement is asking
  for plate tectonics, not drift. The distinction is worth a mark every year.
]

== 3.4 Volcanoes and Earthquakes

Both are consequences of plate movement, and both are concentrated at boundaries — which is
why a map of either is a map of the plate margins.

#v(3pt)

#table(
  columns: (0.95fr, 1.35fr, 1.35fr, 2.2fr),
  inset: (x: 5pt, y: 4.2pt),
  align: (left + horizon, left + horizon, left + horizon, left + horizon),
  stroke: 0.5pt + jamb-border,
  fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { jamb-navy } else if calc.odd(y) { white } else { jamb-light },
  table.header(
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Type],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Lava],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Shape],
    text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 7.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: white)[Example and note],
  ),
  [Shield], [basic, runny], [broad, gently sloping], [Mauna Loa, Hawaii; built by many quiet flows],
  [Composite (strata)], [acidic, viscous], [steep cone of alternating ash and lava], [Fuji, Vesuvius; explosive because the lava traps gas],
  [Caldera], [any type, after collapse], [a wide depression where the summit fell in], [Krakatoa; the magma chamber empties and the roof collapses],
  [Fissure], [basic, in a line], [a plateau of lava rather than a cone], [the Columbia Plateau; the Deccan Traps],
)

#v(3pt)

An earthquake is the shaking produced when accumulated strain in the crust is suddenly
released along a fault. The point of release underground is the *focus*; the point on the
surface directly above it is the *epicentre*. Energy travels as seismic waves: *P waves*
arrive first and push–pull through solids and liquids; *S waves* arrive second and cannot pass
through liquid, which is how the liquid outer core was discovered. The *seismograph* records
the waves; the *Richter scale* measures magnitude, and the *Mercalli scale* measures intensity
of shaking at a place.

#rule-box("The distribution, and the reason for it")[
  About three-quarters of the world's volcanoes and most of its large earthquakes lie on the
  *Ring of Fire* around the Pacific, where oceanic plates are being destroyed. The second great
  belt runs through the Mediterranean and the Himalayas, where continents collide. The third
  follows the mid-ocean ridges, where plates separate. A place far from all three — like most
  of Africa — is seismically quiet, and that is a fact the paper can ask you to explain.
]

== 3.5 Four Worked Questions

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The innermost layer",
  [The innermost layer of the earth is the

    A. mantle \\
    B. outer core \\
    C. inner core \\
    D. crust],
  [
    *Step 1 — order the layers from the surface.* Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core.

    *Step 2 — read "innermost".* The centre of the earth is the inner core, solid because of immense pressure despite its temperature.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* The outer core is liquid and surrounds the inner core; the mantle is above both.

    *Answer: C.*
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "Naming a rock family",
  [Limestone is classified as a sedimentary rock because it

    A. cooled from magma \\
    B. was changed by heat and pressure \\
    C. was formed from the remains of living things \\
    D. is made of large crystals],
  [
    *Step 1 — recall what limestone is.* It is composed mainly of calcium carbonate from the shells and skeletons of marine organisms.

    *Step 2 — match to a family.* Material deposited and compacted, often with fossils, is sedimentary.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* Cooling from magma gives igneous; heat and pressure give metamorphic; large crystals indicate slow cooling underground.

    *Answer: C.*
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "Reading a boundary",
  [The mid-Atlantic ridge is an example of a

    A. destructive plate boundary \\
    B. constructive plate boundary \\
    C. conservative plate boundary \\
    D. transform fault],
  [
    *Step 1 — identify what the ridge is.* A ridge is a line of new ocean floor, built where magma rises between two plates.

    *Step 2 — name the movement.* The plates are moving apart, so crust is created: a constructive (divergent) boundary.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* Destructive boundaries destroy crust at trenches; conservative boundaries slide past without creating crust.

    *Answer: B.*
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "Focus and epicentre",
  [The point on the earth's surface directly above the origin of an earthquake is called the

    A. focus \\
    B. epicentre \\
    C. fault line \\
    D. seismic zone],
  [
    *Step 1 — separate the two points.* The origin underground is the focus; the point on the surface vertically above it is the epicentre.

    *Step 2 — read the stem carefully.* It asks for the point *on the surface*, so the answer is the epicentre.

    *Step 3 — confirm the rivals.* A fault is the break along which movement occurs; a seismic zone is a whole region of activity.

    *Answer: B.*
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

== 3.6 Chapter 3 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The boundary between the crust and the mantle is called the], [Gutenberg discontinuity], [Mohorovičić discontinuity], [Richter discontinuity], [Moho ridge])

#mcq(2, [Which of the following rocks is metamorphic?], [marble], [limestone], [basalt], [sandstone])

#mcq(3, [The theory that explains the movement of continents is], [continental drift], [plate tectonics], [isostasy], [vulcanicity])

#mcq(4, [The instrument used to record earthquake waves is the], [barometer], [hygrometer], [seismograph], [anemometer])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "A", "B", "C"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [The Moho separates crust from mantle; the Gutenberg discontinuity separates mantle from core. The names are swapped in the trap.]),
  ("A", [Marble is limestone that has been changed by heat and pressure. Limestone, basalt and sandstone are sedimentary and igneous rocks respectively.]),
  ("B", [Plate tectonics is the mechanism — plates moving on the asthenosphere. Continental drift is the observation the mechanism explains.]),
  ("C", [A seismograph records ground motion. Barometers measure pressure, hygrometers humidity and anemometers wind speed.]),
))

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_geo1_ch03.svg",
  "Chapter 3 recap card: the layers, the three rocks, the cycle and the evidence.", width: 74%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 3 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    The earth is layered by density: a thin solid crust, a solid but slowly flowing mantle, a
    liquid outer core and a solid inner core, separated at the Moho and the Gutenberg
    discontinuity. The crust is made of three rock families — igneous rock cooled from magma,
    sedimentary rock deposited and compacted in layers, metamorphic rock changed by heat and
    pressure — and the rock cycle moves material from one to another through weathering,
    deposition, burial and melting. The crust is broken into plates that move on the mantle,
    and their boundaries are constructive where new crust is made, destructive where crust is
    destroyed, and conservative where plates slide past. Volcanoes and earthquakes follow those
    boundaries, which is why the Pacific Ring of Fire holds most of both.
  ]
]

